          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Johann Sebastian Bach's Early Life</strong><br>\nJohann Sebastian Bach was born on March 31, 1685, in Eisenach, Germany, into a large family of musicians. His father, Johann Ambrosius Bach, was the town musician, and it was he who taught young Johann to play the violin and the harpsichord. Unfortunately, Bach was orphaned by the age of ten and went to live with his older brother, who introduced him to the organ.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bach's Education</strong><br>\nFor his formal education, Bach attended St. Michael's School in Lüneburg, which was known for its strong musical program. Here, Bach had access to a larger library of music and was exposed to a wider variety of European culture. This exposure helped Bach to develop his style, which was influenced by Italian, French, and German composers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bach as an Organist</strong><br>\nBach's reputation as a skilled organist began to grow when he secured his first job as a church organist in Arnstadt. His skill and technique were so exceptional that he was often asked to test new organs in churches across Germany. However, his sometimes unorthodox and elaborate playing style occasionally drew criticism from church officials.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
