          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Bach's Later Years in Leipzig</strong><br>\nBach's final position was as Cantor at St. Thomas Church in Leipzig, where he was responsible for the music at four churches and the teaching of Latin in the St. Thomas School. In Leipzig, he composed many of his most famous works, such as the \"St. Matthew Passion\" and the \"Mass in B minor.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Legacy of Bach's Music</strong><br>\nAlthough recognized as a great organist in his lifetime, it was not until the 19th century that Bach's compositions gained widespread acclaim. Today, he is regarded as one of the greatest composers of the Baroque era, with an influence that extends well beyond the realm of classical music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bach's Death and Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nJohann Sebastian Bach died on July 28, 1750, after suffering a stroke. His music was revered by those who followed in his footsteps, including the likes of Mozart and Beethoven. In the years following his death, his music was published and studied widely, securing his place as a timeless figure in music history.</p>\n"
              },
